Continuous Integration (CI) with GitHub Actions
1. Continuous Integration (CI)

Continuous Integration (CI) means developers regularly upload code to a shared repository, and the system automatically checks whether the code works correctly.

Purpose of CI
Detect errors early
Automatically run tests
Improve software quality
Save developer time
Avoid integration problems
Example

A student uploads code to GitHub.
GitHub Actions automatically:

builds the project
runs tests
checks for errors

If something fails, the developer gets notified.

2. GitHub Actions

GitHub Actions is a CI/CD tool inside GitHub used to automate software workflows.

It helps automate:

Testing
Building
Deployment
Code checking
Notifications
3. Workflow Automation

Workflow automation means tasks run automatically without manual work.

Example Tasks
Run Java tests automatically
Deploy website automatically
Check coding standards
Send alerts after build completion
4. Workflow Directory Structure

GitHub Actions workflows are stored inside:

.github/workflows/
Example
project-folder/
 └── .github/
      └── workflows/
           └── ci.yml
.yml file contains workflow instructions.
5. Key Components of GitHub Actions
A) Workflow

A workflow is an automated process.

It is written in YAML format.

Example
name: Java CI
B) Jobs

A workflow contains one or more jobs.

Each job performs a task.

Example
Build job
Test job
Deploy job
jobs:
  build:
C) Steps

Each job contains steps.

Steps execute commands one by one.

Example
steps:
  - name: Checkout Code
D) Actions

Actions are reusable code blocks.

GitHub provides many ready-made actions.

Example
uses: actions/checkout@v4

This downloads repository code.

E) Runners

Runners are machines that execute workflows.

GitHub provides:

Ubuntu
Windows
macOS runners
Example
runs-on: ubuntu-latest
6. Workflow Triggers

Triggers decide when workflows should start.

A) Push Trigger

Runs workflow when code is pushed.

on: push
Example

Whenever a developer uploads new code.

B) Pull Request Trigger

Runs when a pull request is created.

on: pull_request

Used to verify code before merging.

C) Schedule Trigger

Runs automatically at fixed times.

on:
  schedule:
    - cron: '0 0 * * *'

Example:

Daily backup
Nightly testing
D) Manual Workflow Trigger

Allows user to start workflow manually.

on:
  workflow_dispatch:

Useful for:

Manual deployments
Special testing
7. Jobs & Matrix Strategies
Jobs

Multiple jobs can run:

sequentially
parallelly
Example
jobs:
  build:
  test:
Matrix Strategy

Matrix strategy runs jobs with multiple configurations.

Example

Test code on:

Java 17
Java 21
strategy:
  matrix:
    java-version: [17, 21]

Benefits:

Saves time
Tests compatibility
8. Steps & Shell Commands

Steps can execute shell commands.

Example
steps:
  - run: mvn test

This runs Maven tests.

Common Commands
Command	Purpose
mvn test	Run tests
npm install	Install packages
npm run build	Build project
java -version	Check Java version
9. Using Marketplace Actions

GitHub Marketplace contains reusable actions created by GitHub and developers.

Examples
Action	Purpose
actions/checkout	Download repository
actions/setup-java	Install Java
actions/setup-node	Install Node.js
Example
uses: actions/setup-java@v4
10. Complete Example Workflow
name: Java CI

on:
  push:
  pull_request:

jobs:
  build:

    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout Code
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Setup Java
        uses: actions/setup-java@v4
        with:
          java-version: '21'

      - name: Build Project
        run: mvn clean install
11. Advantages of GitHub Actions
Easy automation
Integrated with GitHub
Free for small projects
Fast testing
Supports many languages
Large marketplace support
Improves code quality
12. Disadvantages
YAML syntax can be confusing
Complex workflows are difficult
Limited free usage for private repositories
13. Real-Life Uses

Companies use GitHub Actions for:

Automatic testing
Continuous deployment
Docker builds
Cloud deployment
Security scanning

Examples include companies like Microsoft, Google, and Netflix.

